Subject: [#94-2-270] PCR: Recycled Greek Skepticism?

On 24 Feb 1994, Reilly Jones wrote:

> He attacked all conclusions as intellectually indefensible, and bade his
> students be satisfied with probability and the customs of their time."

> 	Pyrrho of Elis around 320 BC - "These opinions were basically three:
> that certainty is unattainable, the wise man will suspend judgment and will seek
> tranquillity rather than truth, and that, since all theories are probably false,
> one might as well accept the myths and conventions of his time and place.

Skepticism contends that certainty is impossible; PCR merely contends 
that certainty is relative.

Skepticism recognized the inevitable failure of justificationism, but 
failed to provide a theory of rationality to replace it which would allow 
one to distinguish between true (more certain) and false (less certain) 
beliefs. Skepticism puts all beliefs on the same footing and assigns them 
all the same truth value; PCR does not. PCR subjects all beliefs to 
criticism, but it does not hold that all beliefs are equally uncertain, 
and it does not assign the same truth value to all beliefs.

> Also, Tim wrote: <Is PCR predicated upon all propositions being not only open
> to, but defeated by criticism?>
> 
> I never saw an answer to this and in light of Carneades' proven prowess in doing
> just this, I think it is an excellent question.

Based on my reading of THE RETREAT TO COMMITMENT, I would have to say 
emphatically that it is not predicated on this idea. 


