> belief in the theory of evolution?" "my justification for my belief
> is that am I critical towards them." This is almost like saying that
> belief in a theory is justified if you take a Popperian approach towards
> the theory.

Um, no. The dialogue would go more like this:

Q: Why do you believe in the theory of evolution?

A: Because I've examined all the alternative theories that I can think of 
and that other people have suggested to me, and they all fall to various 
specific criticisms that the theory of evolution holds up against. 
Creationism lacks explanatory power, because it merely postpones the 
problem ("Where did God come from?"), catastrophic theorie that don't 
take evolutionary change into account are contradicted by experimental 
evidence about mutation, etc. etc. etc.

PCR itself leads to an evolutionary epistemology based on blind, 
random variation and selective retention. Under PCR, we subscribe to 
Theory C because it holds up under criticisms that Theories A, B, and D 
all succumbed to, and because every other theory we've managed to cook up 
and coherently state also fall to criticisms (experimental evidence, 
explanatory power, logical consistency, esthetics ceteribus paribus, 
Ocham's Razor) that Theory C is proof against. When someone comes up with 
an uncomfortable criticism of Theory C *and* a Theory W that withstands 
that criticism, then it's time for a paradigm shift.


