From: Eric Watt Forste <arkuat@netcom.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Feb 1994 04:55:45 -0800 (PST)
Subject: [#94-2-275] PCR: Recycled Greek Skepticism?

On Fri, 25 Feb 1994, Peter Hinssen SH261 8462 wrote:

> > explanatory power, logical consistency, esthetics ceteribus paribus, 
>   ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
>   based on what? Looks like the PCR discussion is turning into the
>   creation Ex Nihilo discussion.

Explanatory power is a personal favorite of mine; Bartley does not stress 
it in tRtC. Basically, if we're trying to figure out the best answer to a 
question, answers that don't even address the question get ruled out 
right away.

You ask "Based on what?"... all I can say is I don't understand your
question. If we're evaluating theories about a phenomenon, clearly a
theory that explains the phenomenon in terms of simpler and
better-understood phenomena is prefereable (ceteribus paribus) to a theory
which "explains" the phenomenon in terms of some equally complex or more
complex or less-well-understood phenomenon, which isn't really an
explanation at all. 

Something that Bartley does talk about in tCtR is the critical check of 
"the problem" rather than explanatory power. This is a more general idea: 
"What problem is the theory intended to solve? Does it do so successfully?"



